WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:07.200
Did you spot the Nazi references in "The Lion&nbsp;
King"? Or the "2001" homage in "Wall-E"? Bowie in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:12.640
"Moana"? These are just a few of the many things&nbsp;
that only adults notice about cartoon villains.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:19.040
The Cartoon Network series "The Powerpuff Girls"&nbsp;
might just be the cutest take ever on superheroes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:25.120
Debuting in 1998, Blossom, Bubbles, and Buttercup&nbsp;
are kindergarten-aged children who protect their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:30.000
town as super-powered beings. The girls have a&nbsp;
great rogue's gallery filled with villains who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:36.320
are often just as brightly-drawn and cute as the&nbsp;
heroines, from Princess Morbucks to Mojo Jojo.

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:43.360
But amidst all that cuteness lies a disturbing&nbsp;
anomaly known as His Infernal Majesty or simply,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:47.360
HIM. This strange red creature&nbsp;
has a soft, seductive voice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:52.800
horn-like ears, a goatee, and thigh-high&nbsp;
boots that look an awful lot like hooves.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:58.160
And his aim isn't world domination, it's to&nbsp;
corrupt the Powerpuff Girls' innocent natures.

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:02.720
In other words, he's Satan. Not exactly what&nbsp;
you usually expect from a kid's cartoon.

00:01:03.671 --> 00:01:08.000
Dr. Heinz Doofenshmirtz is the main villain&nbsp;
of "Phineas and Ferb," which began airing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:14.640
in 2007. A classic mad scientist with improbable&nbsp;
inventive skills, Doofenshmirtz's dearest wish is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:19.360
to conquer the tri-state area. Doofenshmirtz&nbsp;
doesn't have a lot of friends or family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:23.920
but he is deeply attached to his daughter&nbsp;
Vanessa. He can often be seen worrying about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:29.280
her and saving her from trouble, and he even gives&nbsp;
up being evil for her sake in the series finale.

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:33.040
All of that makes Doofenshmirtz one&nbsp;
of the top parents in cartoon history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:38.000
despite being viewed as an evil villain. In&nbsp;
fact, Doofenshmirtz is a far better parent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:42.960
than even Phineas and Ferb's parents. Every&nbsp;
episode of the show has the boys engaging in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:47.600
an outrageous adventure, with no adult around&nbsp;
to stop them from building rollercoasters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:52.800
messing with time travel, engineering giant&nbsp;
robots, or traveling between dimensions.

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:57.520
Seriously, the attitude of Phineas and Ferb's&nbsp;
parents borders on the neglectful — especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:02.160
when compared to the lengths that Doofenshmirtz&nbsp;
goes to while trying to protect Vanessa.

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:05.440
Disney fairytales tend to stay away from lewdness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:11.120
keeping in mind their very young audience. Most&nbsp;
Disney villains are motivated by PG-13 impulses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:15.920
like a desire for magical power or&nbsp;
wealth. Not so with Judge Frollo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:22.640
the main villain of 1996's "The Hunchback of&nbsp;
Notre Dame." Frollo was all kinds of messed up.

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:27.360
As the Minister of Justice, Frollo was&nbsp;
highly disturbed by the unusual passions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:32.320
awakening in his bosom at the sight of the&nbsp;
beautiful Esmeralda. In fact, Frollo sings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:38.080
an entire song, "Hellfire," about how hot he is&nbsp;
for Esmeralda, how his feelings are her fault,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:43.280
and how he will not rest until he gets&nbsp;
with her whether she's into him or not.

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:47.360
It's extremely disturbing watching a&nbsp;
Disney character sing about his desire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:52.000
to assault the heroine. How this film&nbsp;
got a G rating is a mystery to everyone.

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:58.080
Sid Phillips from 1995's "Toy Story" managed to&nbsp;
be a terrifying villain despite only being an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:04.080
11-year-old kid. That was because Sid delighted&nbsp;
in breaking apart toys and reconstructing them in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:10.000
grotesque, horrific ways. The obvious pleasure Sid&nbsp;
takes in mutilating beautiful little toys would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:14.720
sound alarm bells for any psychiatrist,&nbsp;
as well as child protective services.

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:18.880
Sid displays classic signs of a child&nbsp;
who has suffered neglect and abuse.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:24.560
In the movie, we get a brief glimpse of Sid's&nbsp;
father asleep on a reclining chair — a deer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:29.200
head mounted on the wall shows that Mr. Phillips&nbsp;
likes to inflict violence on others. The fact that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:34.240
he is asleep in the middle of the day shows he&nbsp;
is likely jobless, and the many empty beer cans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:39.200
littering the room likely indicate an alcoholic&nbsp;
who doesn't take care of his surroundings.

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:44.080
All these hints, alongside Sid's fear of waking&nbsp;
his father up, point towards the Phillips&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:50.160
household being an unhappy one. How much of Sid's&nbsp;
sadistic impulses are the result of his father may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:55.600
be debatable, but this much is clear: The kid is&nbsp;
seeking an outlet for his anger by hurting toys.

00:03:56.240 --> 00:04:00.800
Disney often makes use of pop culture icons&nbsp;
while creating their animated characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:04.720
a trend that included 2016's "Moana" via Tamatoa,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:09.040
a giant flamboyant crab that&nbsp;
craves shiny, valuable objects.

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:14.800
For older viewers, Tamatoa's look might evoke&nbsp;
memories of iconic glam-rock singer-songwriter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:20.080
David Bowie, and that is no accident. The&nbsp;
look of Tamatoa was in fact based on Bowie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:23.920
and the character's big musical number&nbsp;
"Shiny" was written with Bowie in mind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:28.240
as the film's songwriter Lin-Manuel&nbsp;
Miranda told People in 2016.

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:31.360
"I'd been listening to Bowie&nbsp;
on a loop. I was like, well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:35.600
I'm writing a glam rock tune for this&nbsp;
crab, this jewel-encrusted crab."

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:40.800
To channel the spirit of Bowie further, Tamatoa&nbsp;
was voiced by New Zealand's Jemaine Clement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:43.200
who has been known to bust&nbsp;
out a credible impression&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:47.600
of Bowie in projects like "Flight of the&nbsp;
Conchords." Clement told The AU Review,

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:50.320
"When I tried to record ["Shiny"]&nbsp;
in the character's voice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:56.320
[Miranda] just kept pushing the Bowie thing, do it&nbsp;
more Bowie, he wanted glam, like a T-Rex Bowie."

00:04:56.880 --> 00:05:02.080
Ursula from 1989's "The Little Mermaid" is as&nbsp;
rollickingly entertaining as a Disney villain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:06.880
can get. Which makes sense, because she was&nbsp;
based on a rollickingly entertaining drag queen.

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:10.720
Rob Minkoff, animator for "The Little&nbsp;
Mermaid," revealed in the 2020 Disney+&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:15.440
documentary "Howard" that he came up with a&nbsp;
host of possible designs for Ursula's character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:22.080
including one based on famed drag queen Divine,&nbsp;
best known for playing Edna in 1988's "Hairspray."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:26.160
When the film's lyricist Howard Ashman&nbsp;
saw the drawings, the choice was clear.

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:30.000
"And that design was put on a board with&nbsp;
a bunch of other designs and the day that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:33.680
Howard Ashman came in to look at the&nbsp;
designs he zeroed in on that one."

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:34.560
Truly divine!

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:40.160
1994's "The Lion King" is generally agreed&nbsp;
to be one of the greatest Disney movies of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:45.200
all time. Despite being a story about talking&nbsp;
animals, the movie was grounded in mature themes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:49.840
as it took its cues from the Biblical stories&nbsp;
of Joseph and Moses, William Shakespeare's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:54.960
"Hamlet" — and also from the infamous&nbsp;
Nazi propaganda films of Leni Riefenstahl.

00:05:54.960 --> 00:06:00.240
In the song "Be Prepared," sung by the movie's&nbsp;
main bad guy Scar, we see Hyenas marching past&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:05.200
the villainous lion in a highly regimented style.&nbsp;
The movements of the Hyenas, and the view of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:10.080
their formations, was based specifically&nbsp;
on Nazi propaganda films by Riefenstahl.

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:14.160
According to Entertainment Weekly, the idea&nbsp;
for the song came from a sketch by story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:19.360
staffer Jorgen Klubien that pictured Scar as&nbsp;
Hitler, which inspired the directors to craft&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:23.520
the "Be Prepared" sequence, which became&nbsp;
one of the most iconic parts of the film.

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:28.320
Pixar is a studio that excels in telling&nbsp;
stories filled with warmth and empathy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:33.440
Stanley Kubrick was a filmmaker renowned for&nbsp;
telling stories filled with tension, coldness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:39.040
and dread. The two might not seem to have much&nbsp;
of an overlap at first blush, but 2008's "Wall-E"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.040 --> 00:06:43.360
brought the two together in an unexpected manner&nbsp;
through the introduction of its main villain.

00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:47.920
In "Wall-E," the central antagonist&nbsp;
is a rogue A.I. called Auto that has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.920 --> 00:06:52.560
control of a vessel carrying humanity&nbsp;
into the furthest reaches of space.

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:58.560
Auto's looks and personality are clearly based&nbsp;
on the villainous HAL 9000 from Kubrick's "2001:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:03.360
A Space Odyssey." But the references&nbsp;
don't end there. A scene from "2001"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:08.000
where an ape learns to use tools features&nbsp;
the same music as the scene in "Wall-E"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:12.320
when the Captain of Auto's space vessel&nbsp;
finally leaves his chair to stand on his feet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.320 --> 00:07:17.760
learning self-reliance in the process after having&nbsp;
depended on Auto for everything for so many years.

00:07:18.400 --> 00:07:22.800
2005's Disney film "Chicken Little"&nbsp;
was a heartfelt story about growing up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:27.200
standing up for your beliefs, and learning to&nbsp;
let your children become self-reliant. And it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.200 --> 00:07:33.920
also paid homage to one of the most influential,&nbsp;
terrifying sci-fi novels of all time, H.G. Wells'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.920 --> 00:07:40.480
1897 masterpiece "The War of the Worlds," which&nbsp;
features aliens landing in terrifying tripods.

00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:44.800
Fortunately, the machines in "Chicken Little"&nbsp;
were a lot more benign than the ones that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.800 --> 00:07:48.880
showed up in "The War of the Worlds." It&nbsp;
is revealed in the final act of the movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.880 --> 00:07:53.760
that the aliens initially visited Earth just to&nbsp;
gather acorns, and later returned with an entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:58.480
armada because they thought one of their&nbsp;
own was being held hostage by Earthlings.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.480 --> 00:08:02.800
Once the misunderstanding is cleared up, the&nbsp;
aliens return peacefully to their home planet.

00:08:03.440 --> 00:08:07.440
For multiple generations of children, the&nbsp;
"Tom and Jerry" cartoons have been a popular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.440 --> 00:08:12.720
retelling of the classic "David vs. Goliath"&nbsp;
narrative. In this case, the big, dim-witted cat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:17.280
Tom is the evil Goliath, while the smaller,&nbsp;
quick-witted Jerry is plucky little David,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:22.640
who humbles the giant with his brains and will&nbsp;
power. But is the story really that simple?

00:08:22.640 --> 00:08:25.840
Once you grow up and need to look&nbsp;
into buying or renting your own house,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.840 --> 00:08:29.520
you begin to realize what a major&nbsp;
problem house pests like mice can be,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.520 --> 00:08:34.880
which is why house pets like cats are used&nbsp;
to eliminate pests and keep the home safe.

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:38.640
Once you start looking at the situation from&nbsp;
that angle, you realize that Jerry was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.640 --> 00:08:43.840
villain of the show, an uninvited house pest&nbsp;
that ran roughshod all over the house eating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.840 --> 00:08:48.640
and taking whatever he wanted. Meanwhile, Tom&nbsp;
was simply the house pet trying to do what it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.640 --> 00:08:54.080
had been trained to accomplish, keeping the home&nbsp;
safe from unwanted rodents. Sure, Tom was always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.080 --> 00:08:59.120
left frustrated after his encounters with Jerry —&nbsp;
but just imagine how Tom's owners must have felt.

00:08:59.120 --> 00:09:02.320
So if you think Jerry is the&nbsp;
good guy, all we can say is

00:09:02.320 --> 00:09:06.634
"Don't you believe it!!"

00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:10.640
Adult innuendo in cartoons aimed&nbsp;
at children is not a new thing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.640 --> 00:09:15.280
But sometimes the innuendo is so in-your-face&nbsp;
you can't help but be surprised by the audacity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.280 --> 00:09:19.040
of the show's writers. Case in point,&nbsp;
Roxy Rocket's appearance in "The New&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.040 --> 00:09:22.800
Batman Adventures," where she had a&nbsp;
singular obsession with getting laid.

00:09:22.800 --> 00:09:26.480
In the episode "The Ultimate Thrill,"&nbsp;
viewers are introduced to Roxy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.480 --> 00:09:31.840
a daredevil stuntwoman who turned to a life of&nbsp;
crime to satisfy her craving for excitement.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.840 --> 00:09:35.680
In Roxy's mind, her reaction to&nbsp;
danger is quite similar to arousal.

00:09:35.680 --> 00:09:41.040
Roxy's weapon of choice is a giant, phallic&nbsp;
rocket, and she soon becomes obsessed with Batman.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.040 --> 00:09:45.280
Throughout the episode, Roxy makes one charged&nbsp;
comment after another, telling Penguin about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.280 --> 00:09:50.240
Batman, quote, "I was the best he ever had,"&nbsp;
and, "I love a man with staying power."

00:09:50.240 --> 00:09:55.600
But the most brazen scene comes at the end. When&nbsp;
Roxy's last daring heist is stopped by Batman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.600 --> 00:09:58.240
she appears to be in the throes of ecstasy.

00:09:58.240 --> 00:10:03.840
"Me and you! Yeah, yeah, YEAH!!!"

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:09.200
2010's "Despicable Me" had you rooting&nbsp;
for the bad guy. Felonious Gru was an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.200 --> 00:10:13.600
aspiring super-villain who dreamed of pulling&nbsp;
off the ultimate heist to prove himself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.600 --> 00:10:17.040
So he was forced to go to the Bank&nbsp;
of Evil, an organization that offers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.040 --> 00:10:21.120
loans to supervillains who can prove&nbsp;
their schemes are sufficiently evil.

00:10:21.120 --> 00:10:24.160
However, the entrance to the&nbsp;
bank is hidden behind a urinal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.160 --> 00:10:28.960
But the truly dastardly touch is that&nbsp;
the entrance is behind the middle urinal.

00:10:28.960 --> 00:10:32.240
When you go to a public restroom,&nbsp;
most people's instinct is to use the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.240 --> 00:10:36.800
urinal to the left or the right, because that&nbsp;
minimizes the chance of having to pee standing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.800 --> 00:10:42.080
next to someone. Only someone truly evil and&nbsp;
twisted would willingly use the middle urinal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.080 --> 00:10:46.480
where he could be flanked on both&nbsp;
sides by others. It is exactly that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.480 --> 00:10:50.720
kind of a dastardly mind that the Bank of Evil&nbsp;
would be interested in doing business with.

00:10:52.145 --> 00:10:55.920
The Evil Queen from 1937's "Snow White and&nbsp;
the Seven Dwarfs" might just be the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.920 --> 00:11:01.360
iconic Disney villain of all time. She's often&nbsp;
portrayed as the personification of evil, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.360 --> 00:11:06.800
adults might be more likely to diagnose her as&nbsp;
suffering from Narcissistic Personality Disorder.

00:11:06.800 --> 00:11:12.720
NPD, also referred to as Megalomania, is a mental&nbsp;
condition which has been officially recognized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.720 --> 00:11:18.080
by the American Psychiatric Association. People&nbsp;
suffering from NPD have an exaggerated sense of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.080 --> 00:11:23.760
their own importance, as well as a deep need for&nbsp;
excessive attention and admiration. This in turn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.760 --> 00:11:28.800
leads to the sufferer having trouble maintaining&nbsp;
relationships due to a lack of empathy for others.

00:11:28.800 --> 00:11:34.240
These symptoms fit the Evil Queen to a T. The&nbsp;
tragic part is, NPD is actually treatable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.240 --> 00:11:37.520
but the people who suffer from it have&nbsp;
such a high opinion of themselves that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.520 --> 00:11:41.280
they refuse to recognize they have&nbsp;
a problem that requires therapy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.280 --> 00:11:44.960
The Evil Queen's time could have been&nbsp;
put to much better use if she had stopped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.960 --> 00:11:50.000
obsessing over Snow White so much and booked an&nbsp;
appointment with the Dwarf named "Doc" instead.

